[{"id":"question 1185","verdict":"wrong","correct_option":3,"evidence":"The standard supports up to 16 CBR ranges for sidelink congestion control; the lookup table is described as 'up to 16 CBR ranges' in 3GPP working assumptions (R1-2004663) and confirmed by tutorial papers and patent literature on NR V2X mode 2.","source":"3GPP TS 38.214 §8.1.6; 3GPP RAN1#101 R1-2004663 (working assumption 'Up to 16 CBR ranges are supported'); patent CN114071430B"},{"id":"question 1196","verdict":"wrong","correct_option":3,"evidence":"The integration of 5GS into TSN is defined in 3GPP specs (TS 23.501 architecture clauses 4.4.8/5.27/5.28/Annex H/I; TS 23.502 procedures Annex F). IEEE 802.1Q defines VLAN/bridging generally, not the 5G-TSN integration. TS 23.540 is about SMS, not TSN. TS 23.501 is not in the options, leaving TS 23.502 (option 3) as the closest valid choice.","source":"3GPP news article '5G for Industry 4.0' (lists TS 23.501 clauses and TS 23.502 Annex F for TSN); IEEE 802.1Q defines TSN bridges (not 5G integration); TS 23.540 = Service Based SMS"},{"id":"question 1226","verdict":"correct","correct_option":2,"evidence":"Per TS 23.288 / patent US20250185086A1: 'If the authorization check is successful, the SMF may subscribe to the analytics information... if the SMF has not already subscribed to [Analytics ID] analytics for other UEs and/or itself'. The SMF checks existing subscriptions before subscribing to avoid duplicates.","source":"3GPP TS 23.288 (UE analytics exposure procedure); US patent 20250185086A1 §0052"},{"id":"question 1230","verdict":"correct","correct_option":4,"evidence":"IEEE 802.11 standard defines 'registered location secure server (RLSS): An entity that accesses and manages a database that organizes storage of information by geographic location and securely holds the location and some operating parameters of one or more basic service sets (BSSs).'","source":"IEEE Std 802.11-2020 definitions; CN103918346B"},{"id":"question 1241","verdict":"correct","correct_option":5,"evidence":"The TRS availability indication (e.g., via paging DCI / PEI bits or DCI format 2_5 for IAB) uses bits that indicate whether TRS resource sets are present/available; a '1' bit indicates the TRS resource set is present (available for use).","source":"3GPP TS 38.213 / 38.331; EP4412137A1 (TRS availability indication bits = presence per resource set)"},{"id":"question 1244","verdict":"correct","correct_option":2,"evidence":"3GPP TS 28.105 defines 'AI decision entity: an entity that applies a non-ML based logic for making AI decisions that can be managed as a single composite entity.' Option 2 matches this definition (minor wording difference: 'decisions' vs 'AI decisions').","source":"3GPP TS 28.105 §3.1"},{"id":"question 1258","verdict":"correct","correct_option":4,"evidence":"TS 26.348 / TS 29.116 define xMB as the 'Northbound Application Programming Interface (API) for MBMS at the xMB reference point' that allows content providers to provision and control broadcast services (i.e., control MBMS delivery procedures).","source":"3GPP TS 26.348 (xMB API); TS 29.116 §5.1.0 'xMB API is a RESTful API that allows Content Providers to provision broadcast services'"},{"id":"question 1272","verdict":"correct","correct_option":1,"evidence":"IEEE 802.11af defines RLSS as 'An entity that accesses and manages a database that organizes storage of information by geographic location and securely holds the location and some operating parameters of one or more basic service sets (BSSs)'; the role of RLSS is to provide channel-use / BSS information (derived from GDB) to GDD-enabling STAs. The marked option is the closest match in wording to this role.","source":"IEEE 802.11af (RLSS definition); arXiv 1712.05003 §IEEE 802.11af architecture"},{"id":"question 1287","verdict":"correct","correct_option":5,"evidence":"TS 38.401 §8.1: 'The gNB-DU includes the RRC message and, if the UE is admitted, the corresponding low layer configuration for the UE in the INITIAL UL RRC MESSAGE TRANSFER message and transfers to the gNB-CU.'","source":"3GPP TS 38.401 §8.1 (UE Initial Access procedure, step 2)"},{"id":"question 1295","verdict":"correct","correct_option":2,"evidence":"IEEE 802.15.4: 'If a beacon frame is received when macAutoRequest is set to FALSE, each recorded PAN descriptor is sent to the next higher layer in a separate MLME-BEACON-NOTIFY.indication primitive.'","source":"IEEE 802.15.4-2003/2006 §7.5.2.1.2 / 7.1.5.1"},{"id":"question 1299","verdict":"correct","correct_option":1,"evidence":"TR 21.915 §9.4.2 (MBMS_Mcservices summary): 'Multi-server bearer coordination: To avoid allocating duplicate bearers for an MBMS service area, a single MC service server may manage all the MBMS media transmission for all groups and users within a particular MBMS service area.' Specified in TS 23.280.","source":"3GPP TR 21.915 §9.4.2; TS 23.280"},{"id":"question 1309","verdict":"correct","correct_option":2,"evidence":"TS 36.101 §4.4: 'some RF requirements in later releases may be mandatory independent of whether the UE supports the bands specified in later releases or not. The set of requirements from later releases that is also mandatory for UEs conforming to the 3GPP release of the present document is determined by regional regulation.'","source":"3GPP TS 36.101 §4.4 (RF requirements in later releases)"},{"id":"question 1330","verdict":"correct","correct_option":4,"evidence":"All VLANs (regardless of organization) limit the scope of broadcast messages by creating separate Layer-2 broadcast domains. The fundamental purpose of any VLAN is broadcast domain segmentation, so all VLANs limit broadcast problems.","source":"IEEE 802.1Q (VLAN standard); general networking references (Cisco, etc.)"},{"id":"question 1355","verdict":"correct","correct_option":4,"evidence":"TR 21.914 (S8HR summary, V8 work item): 'Core network aspects (CT Work Item \"V8-CT\"). Most of the CT groups work was to add the new procedure to fetch and provide necessary information to establish emergency call.' This matches the marked option.","source":"3GPP TR 21.914 S8HR summary; 3GPP work item 730024 (V8-CT) defined by CP-160479"},{"id":"question 1380","verdict":"correct","correct_option":2,"evidence":"TS 23.501 §5.21.2.3: 'In the case that an AMF fails and the 5G-AN/peer CP NFs detect that the AMF has failed... following actions are taken: ... 5G-AN marks this AMF as failed and not consider the AMF for selection until explicitly notified.'","source":"3GPP TS 23.501 §5.21.2.3 (AMF Auto-recovery)"},{"id":"question 1411","verdict":"correct","correct_option":1,"evidence":"TS 23.288 / TR 23.700-81: 'the NWDAF containing MTLF may consider the rating of untrusted AF(s) when used as data sources'. The MTLF considers the rating of untrusted AFs.","source":"3GPP TS 23.700-81 §6.34.2.2; US patent CN119895831A (rating of untrusted AF data sources)"},{"id":"question 1426","verdict":"correct","correct_option":2,"evidence":"TS 22.261 §7.11 KPIs for tactile and multi-modal communication service define delay-difference thresholds across media components (e.g., visual-tactile sync <15/50ms, haptic 5ms, etc.). TR 23.700-60 §6.1 also emphasizes coordination of QoS across flows: 'The delay difference between two flows should be less than some values'. Synchronization between different media components is the key requirement.","source":"3GPP TS 22.261 §7.11; TR 23.700-60 §6.1 (tactile and multi-modal communication service)"},{"id":"question 1445","verdict":"correct","correct_option":4,"evidence":"TS 23.548 §5.1.2: 'EASDF Discovery and Selection: The EASDF discovery and selection is defined in clause 6.3 in TS 23.501.' The interactions between 5GC NF(s) and the EASDF take place within a PLMN for the purpose of EASDF discovery and selection (EASDF registers to NRF, SMF selects EASDF).","source":"3GPP TS 23.548 §5.1.2; TS 23.501 §6.3"}]